  "preamble": "In central and eastern Old French the diphthong ei (from Latin tonic ē and ĭ in open syllables) backed and rounded its nucleus to oi in the 12th century; Norman and other western/northern dialects (and Anglo-Norman) kept ei. The oi then developed to [we] and [wa] in later French (roi, moi, toile). The change is otherwise rare.",
